  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how different metallic nanostructures concentrate and direct electromagnetic fields. It presents four common geometries—nanosphere, nanowire, nanodisk, and nanogaps—and highlights how shape controls the surrounding field distribution and polarization response.\n\nFor the nanosphere, curved field lines wrap symmetrically around the particle, suggesting a more isotropic dipole-like response in a medium labeled with dielectric constant. In contrast, the elongated nanowire and flattened nanodisk show strongly shape-dependent behavior: arrows indicate polarization across the short dimension, while dashed arrows along the long in-plane axis suggest charge or plasmon motion extending through the structure. Bright yellow regions appear at edges or ends, emphasizing localized field enhancement at sharp boundaries.\n\nThe nanogap example shows the strongest confinement. A small dielectric spacer between two metallic regions creates an intense field concentrated inside the narrow gap, with the yellow region pinched between the facing surfaces. This visually communicates the key idea that reducing separation between metallic elements dramatically increases local electromagnetic intensity.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that nanostructure geometry—from symmetric particles to anisotropic rods, disks, and especially coupled gaps—governs where fields localize and how strongly light can be concentrated at the nanoscale.",
